  "worked_solution": "First, let's round the amounts of flax oil and seed:\n\n- Oil: $315⟶300$\n\n- Seed: $945⟶900$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the amount of flax oil to the amount of flaxseed (expressed as a fraction) is approximately\n\n$$\n\\frac{300}{900}.\n$$\n\nSo the numerator represents the amount of flax oil and the denominator represents the amount of flaxseed.\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{300÷100}{900÷100}=\\frac{3}{9}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3÷3}{9÷3}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $600$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $200$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\times 200}{3\\times 200}=\\frac{200}{600}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $200$ grams of flax oil can be obtained from $600$ grams of flaxseed.",
